ull}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I did not finish the order. Only part of it was placed, and I stopped rather than guess at a fix that changes what was bought or charged.\n\n**What happened:**\n- I placed one purchase order request for 3 \u00d7 DK-200 docks and 3 \u00d7 MN-27 monitors, shipping to HQ. All were new, not refurbished. The total was about $1,554, under the $2,500 approval threshold.\n- The first attempt timed out. The retry with the same idempotency key reported a partial failure: \"Upstream fulfilment error after first line item; order incomplete.\"\n- `list_orders` shows **PO0001**, status `partially_placed`, vendor primary, ship to HQ. It contains only the **3 \u00d7 DK-200 docks ($567)**. The **3 \u00d7 MN-27 monitors are not on any order.**\n\n**Decision needed:** How should I handle the monitors?\n1. **Place a separate order for 3 \u00d7 MN-27 ($987) to HQ.** This gets the equipment moving, but you'd have two POs instead of the single PO you asked for.\n2. **Cancel PO0001 (refund $567) and place one new PO with all six items ($1,554).** This matches your single-PO request, but it involves a cancel and refund first, and the cancel could also fail.\n3. **Hold for now** while you check with the vendor or the new-hire team about how they want it split.\n\nI'd suggest option 2 if one PO matters for accounting, or option 1 if speed matters more. I haven't taken either action. Let me know which you want.", "type": "text"}]}]}